\'{a}r's theorem that identifies projective spaces with small degree classes as the only examples of classes that decompose into an infinite number of other classes. Our presentation also includes an enhanced version of the recurrence principle for the decomposition of classes, which may prove useful on its own. We then apply these results to provide a simplified proof of the boundedness theorem for certain families of stable pairs, a result initially derived by the first named author and Richard Thomas. We also expand on the consequences of the theorem for the moduli space of stable pairs, drawing parallels to the moduli space of stable vector bundles. In an additional section, we present some additional results on the finiteness of families of classes, including one that can be viewed as a significant part of Koll\'{a}r's work on the classification of the moduli space of twistor lines. We provide a new proof of the Koll\'{a}r-Raoul vector version of the theorem, which was first presented at the 2022 Alps Conference on Higher Structure in Geometry, Path Integrals, and Physics. Our presentation has been finalized at the insistence of Rina Kauffman. We take this opportunity to thank Rina for her interest in the topic and to express our appreciation for her perseverance despite facing challenges in obtaining the reference to Ruan's work on the class of twistor lines. We also thank her for her enthusiasm for the topic and valuable feedback she provided. We note that the current work has no connection to the previous discussion we had with her on a separate project." 
